e is identified.\n\t•\tMuscle fibers are divided.\n\t•\tThe mucosa is preserved.\n\t•\tOften combined with an anti-reflux procedure.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tUpper GI endoscopy\n\t•\tBarium swallow\n\t•\tEsophageal manometry\n\t•\tCT scan\n\t•\tBiopsy when malignancy is suspected\n\nManagement\n\t•\tTreat underlying cause\n\t•\tDietary modification\n\t•\tEndoscopic dilatation when appropriate\n\t•\tSurgery for severe structural/motility disease\n\t•\tNutritional support\n\t•\tSwallowing rehabilitation\n\nComplications\n\t•\tEsophageal perforation\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tAspiration\n\t•\tGERD\n\t•\tAnastomotic leak after major reconstruction\n\t•\tRecurrent dysphagia\n\n⸻\n\n⭐ Quick Revision\n\nEmbolectomy → removal of embolus\nVascular reconstruction → repair/replacement/bypass of vessel\nAneurysm surgery → repair of dilated weak vessel\nAmputation → removal of non-viable limb\nGangrene surgery → removal of dead/infected tissue\nDVT → clot in deep vein\nPulmonary embolism → clot blocks pulmonary artery\nKidney transplant → replacement of failed kidney\nPortal hypertension → increased portal venous pressure\nEsophageal surgery → treatment of structural/motility causes of dysphagia\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-10T19:31:05+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":8,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:01:06Z","content":"Burns Plastic Surgery S Das BPT Exam Notes Easy language moderately explained Note These are simplified exam oriented notes based on standard surgical concepts covered in S Das They are paraphrased rather than copied verbatim --- **1 BURNS** **Definition** A burn is an injury to the skin and sometimes deeper tissues caused by heat chemicals electricity radiation or friction **Etiology** • Thermal flame hot liquid steam hot objects • Chemical acids and alkalis • Electrical electrical current • Radiation sunlight radiotherapy etc • Friction skin injury due to rubbing **Indications for Hospital / Surgical Management** • Deep or extensive burns • Burns involving face hands feet genitalia major joints • Circumferential burns • Electrical or chemical burns • Inhalation injury • Burns requiring skin grafting **Contraindications** There are no absolute contraindications to emergency burn treatment Definitive surgery may be delayed if the patient is haemodynamically unstable or medically unfit **Risk Factors** • Children and elderly people • Occupational exposure to fire / chemicals • Smoking and unsafe cooking practices • Poor electrical safety • Lack of protective equipment **Types** According to depth 1 Superficial burn epidermis involved red and painful 2 Partial thickness burn epidermis and part of dermis involved blistering and pain 3 Full thickness burn entire skin thickness destroyed skin may be white brown or charred and sensation may be reduced 4 Deep burn extends into deeper tissues such as fat muscle or bone According to cause thermal chemical electrical and radiation burns **Investigation** • Estimate percentage of total body surface area TBSA burned • Assess burn depth • Vital signs and urine output • CBC electrolytes renal function • ABG and carboxyhaemoglobin when inhalation injury is suspected • ECG in electrical burns • X ray / other imaging when associated injuries are suspected **Management** Immediate management ABCDE • A Airway assessment early intubati\n<truncated 44615 bytes>\nticulum • Severe reflux related narrowing • Congenital abnormalities **Indications** • Severe achalasia • Esophageal obstruction • Cancer • Severe stricture • Failure of conservative treatment **Contraindications** Depend on the specific operation • Severe uncorrectable medical illness • Active uncontrolled infection • Patient unfit for anesthesia **Risk Factors** • Smoking • Alcohol • GERD • Obesity • Achalasia • Previous esophageal injury **Types of Surgery** 1 Heller myotomy commonly for achalasia 2 POEM endoscopic muscle cutting procedure for achalasia 3 Esophageal dilatation for strictures 4 Esophagectomy removal of part / all of the esophagus usually for cancer 5 Esophageal reconstruction restoring food passage after esophageal removal **Surgical Procedure Heller Myotomy** • Small abdominal / chest incisions are made • The tight lower esophageal muscle is identified • Muscle fibers are divided • The mucosa is preserved • Often combined with an anti reflux procedure **Investigations** • Upper GI endoscopy • Barium swallow • Esophageal manometry • CT scan • Biopsy when malignancy is suspected **Management** • Treat underlying cause • Dietary modification • Endoscopic dilatation when appropriate • Surgery for severe structural / motility disease • Nutritional support • Swallowing rehabilitation **Complications** • Esophageal perforation • Bleeding • Infection • Aspiration • GERD • Anastomotic leak after major reconstruction • Recurrent dysphagia --- **Quick Revision** Embolectomy removal of embolus Vascular reconstruction repair / replacement / bypass of vessel Aneurysm surgery repair of dilated weak vessel Amputation removal of non viable limb Gangrene surgery removal of dead / infected tissue DVT clot in deep vein Pulmonary embolism clot blocks pulmonary artery Kidney transplant replacement of failed kidney Portal 