{"step_index":9,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:05:45Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nAbsolutely. Here are Thoracic Surgeries in the same order, using simple, exam-oriented language.\n\nTHORACIC SURGERIES\n\n1. Thoracoplasty\n\nDefinition\n\nThoracoplasty is a surgical procedure in which part of the ribs is removed to collapse the chest wall over a diseased part of the lung, helping the lung cavity to close.\n\nEtiology / Indications\n\t•\tOld pulmonary tuberculosis with persistent cavity\n\t•\tChronic empyema\n\t•\tBronchopleural fistula\n\t•\tPersistent infected pleural cavity\n\t•\tDestroyed lung when other procedures are unsuitable\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tSevere respiratory insufficiency\n\t•\tExtensive bilateral lung disease\n\t•\tPatient unfit for major surgery\n\t•\tActive uncontrolled infection\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tChronic pulmonary TB\n\t•\tPoor nutrition\n\t•\tChronic infection\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tPrevious thoracic surgery\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tComplete thoracoplasty – extensive rib removal.\n\t2.\tPartial thoracoplasty – only selected ribs are removed.\n\t3.\tApical thoracoplasty – mainly upper ribs are removed to collapse the apical cavity.\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tIncision is made over the chest wall.\n\t•\tSelected ribs are exposed.\n\t•\tPortions of ribs are removed.\n\t•\tChest wall collapses inward.\n\t•\tThe underlying cavity is compressed.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tPulmonary function tests\n\t•\tSputum examination\n\t•\tBlood investigations\n\nManagement\n\t•\tAnti-tubercular treatment when indicated\n\t•\tAntibiotics for infection\n\t•\tPain management\n\t•\tRespiratory physiotherapy\n\t•\tBreathing exercises\n\t•\tGradual mobilization\n\nComplications\n\t•\tChest deformity\n\t•\tReduced chest expansion\n\t•\tRestrictive respiratory impairment\n\t•\tPain\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tPoor shoulder movement\n\n⸻\n\n2. Pulmonary Resection Surgery\n\nDefinition\n\nPulmonary resection is surgery in which a diseased portion of the lung is removed.\n\nEtiology / Indications\n\t•\tLung cancer\n\t•\tBronchiectasis\n\t•\tLung abscess\n\t•\tLocalized tuberculosis\n\t•\tPulmonary cysts\n\t•\tLocalized destroyed \n<truncated 13754 bytes>\nlignant pleural disease\n\t•\tChronic pleural infection in selected cases\n\nContraindications\n\t•\tSevere respiratory/cardiac disease\n\t•\tPatient unfit for surgery\n\t•\tExtensive disease where surgery is not beneficial\n\nRisk Factors\n\t•\tRecurrent pneumothorax\n\t•\tSmoking\n\t•\tPleural malignancy\n\t•\tChronic pleural infection\n\t•\tPrevious thoracic disease\n\nTypes\n\t1.\tPartial pleurectomy – part of pleura is removed.\n\t2.\tTotal pleurectomy – extensive removal of pleura.\n\t3.\tPleurectomy/decortication – pleura and fibrous peel are removed in selected diseases.\n\nSurgical Procedure\n\t•\tChest is accessed, often using VATS or open thoracotomy.\n\t•\tDiseased pleura is identified.\n\t•\tPleura is carefully separated and removed.\n\t•\tChest drains are placed.\n\t•\tChest is closed.\n\nInvestigations\n\t•\tChest X-ray\n\t•\tCT chest\n\t•\tPleural fluid analysis\n\t•\tPleural biopsy\n\t•\tPFT\n\t•\tBlood tests\n\nManagement\n\t•\tPain control\n\t•\tChest drainage\n\t•\tBreathing exercises\n\t•\tIncentive spirometry\n\t•\tSupported coughing\n\t•\tEarly mobilization\n\t•\tTreatment of underlying disease\n\nComplications\n\t•\tBleeding\n\t•\tAir leak\n\t•\tInfection\n\t•\tPersistent pain\n\t•\tRespiratory complications\n\t•\tReduced lung expansion\n\n⸻\n\n⭐ Quick Revision\n\t1.\tThoracoplasty → removal of ribs to collapse chest wall/cavity\n\t2.\tPulmonary resection → removal of diseased lung tissue\n\t3.\tThoracotomy → surgical opening of chest\n\t4.\tLobectomy → removal of one lung lobe\n\t5.\tPneumonectomy → removal of entire lung\n\t6.\tDecortication → removal of fibrous pleural peel\n\t7.\tPneumothorax surgery → treat persistent/recurrent air leak\n\t8.\tHydropneumothorax surgery → drain air + fluid and treat cause\n\t9.\tEmpyema surgery → drain pus ± decortication\n\t10.\tTB surgery → remove/manage localized destroyed or complicated lung disease\n\t11.\tLung transplant → replacement of diseased lung(s)\n\t12.\tPleurectomy → removal of pleura\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-10T19:35:45+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
