{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:28:34Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n1. ARTHRODESIS Definition Arthrodesis is a surgical procedure in which a joint is deliberately fused by removing articular cartilage and stabilizing the joint until bony union occurs, permanently eliminating movement at that joint. Objectives • Relieve severe pain • Provide a stable joint • Correct deformity • Improve function • Prevent further joint destruction Indications • Severe osteoarthritis or rheumatoid arthritis • Post-traumatic arthritis • Joint destruction after trauma • Selected controlled infective or tuberculous arthritis • Severe joint instability • Paralytic instability • Congenital deformity • Failed reconstruction or joint replacement • When joint replacement is unsuitable Contraindications • Active uncontrolled infection • Poor vascularity • Severe osteoporosis • Poor general health preventing surgery • Inadequate soft-tissue coverage • Severe disease of adjacent joints Classification • By site: spinal, shoulder, elbow, wrist, hip, knee, ankle and small-joint arthrodesis • By technique: extra-articular, intra-articular and combined • By fixation: cast/immobilization, internal fixation, external fixation or combined Principles • Adequate exposure • Remove diseased cartilage • Prepare subchondral bone • Correct deformity • Position joint in a functional position • Obtain good bone-to-bone contact • Provide stable fixation• Use bone graft when required • Preserve blood supply • Immobilize until solid union General surgical procedure • Expose joint • Remove cartilage and diseased tissue • Freshen bone surfaces • Correct deformity • Position surfaces in functional alignment • Insert bone graft when indicated • Fix with screws, plates, nails or other fixation • Close wound and immobilize Postoperative & physiotherapy management • Pain and edema control • Wound care and radiographic monitoring • Respiratory/circulatory exercises when indicated • Active movement of uninvolved joints • Isometric ex\n<truncated 13043 bytes>\nvs incomplete amputation • By level: finger, hand, wrist, forearm, elbow, arm; toe, foot, ankle, leg, knee, thigh • By mechanism: clean-cut, crush, avulsion and combined crush-avulsion Principles • Resuscitate and control bleeding • Preserve amputated part correctly • Debride nonviable tissue • Prepare/shorten bone when required • Restore skeletal stability• Repair tendons • Repair arteries and veins • Repair nerves • Provide soft-tissue coverage • Monitor circulation closely Preservation of amputated part • Wrap in sterile moist gauze • Place in a clean waterproof bag • Place the bag in an ice/cold-water mixture • Do not place the amputated part directly on ice or directly in water Surgical sequence • Debridement • Bone preparation and fixation • Tendon repair • Arterial microsurgical anastomosis • Venous repair • Nerve repair • Skin/soft-tissue coverage • Postoperative vascular monitoring Physiotherapy management • Protect repairs and control edema • Maintain safe-joint mobility • Respiratory/circulatory exercises • Appropriate isometrics • Progressive ROM after healing • Tendon-gliding where appropriate • Strengthening • Sensory re-education • Scar and edema management • Fine-motor training for upper limb • Gait training for lower limb • Proprioception and functional/return-to-work training Complications • Vascular thrombosis • Arterial occlusion • Venous congestion • Infection • Compartment syndrome • Tissue necrosis• Joint stiffness • Muscle weakness • Tendon adhesions • Non-union/malunion • Cold intolerance • Neuroma • Poor sensory/motor recovery • Chronic pain • Functional limitation Conclusion Limb replantation is a complex multidisciplinary microsurgical procedure. Outcome depends on injury level and mechanism, ischaemia time, tissue viability, quality of repair and prolonged rehabilitation.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T15:58:34+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
