{"step_index":7,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:28:34Z","content":"**1 ARTHRODESIS** **Definition** Arthrodesis is a surgical procedure in which a joint is deliberately fused by removing articular cartilage and stabilizing the joint until bony union occurs permanently eliminating movement at that joint **Objectives** • Relieve severe pain • Provide a stable joint • Correct deformity • Improve function • Prevent further joint destruction **Indications** • Severe osteoarthritis or rheumatoid arthritis • Post traumatic arthritis • Joint destruction after trauma • Selected controlled infective or tuberculous arthritis • Severe joint instability • Paralytic instability • Congenital deformity • Failed reconstruction or joint replacement • When joint replacement is unsuitable **Contraindications** • Active uncontrolled infection • Poor vascularity • Severe osteoporosis • Poor general health preventing surgery • Inadequate soft tissue coverage • Severe disease of adjacent joints **Classification** • By site spinal shoulder elbow wrist hip knee ankle and small joint arthrodesis • By technique extra articular intra articular and combined • By fixation cast/immobilization internal fixation external fixation or combined **Principles** • Adequate exposure • Remove diseased cartilage • Prepare subchondral bone • Correct deformity • Position joint in a functional position • Obtain good bone to bone contact • Provide stable fixation • Use bone graft when required • Preserve blood supply • Immobilize until solid union **General surgical procedure** • Expose joint • Remove cartilage and diseased tissue • Freshen bone surfaces • Correct deformity • Position surfaces in functional alignment • Insert bone graft when indicated • Fix with screws plates nails or other fixation • Close wound and immobilize **Postoperative and physiotherapy management** • Pain and edema control • Wound care and radiographic monitoring • Respiratory/circulatory exercises when indicated • Active movement of uninvolved joints • Isome\n<truncated 13081 bytes>\nciated injuries • Unsuitable amputated part • Severe medical instability **Classification** • Complete vs incomplete amputation • By level finger hand wrist forearm elbow arm toe foot ankle leg knee thigh • By mechanism clean cut crush avulsion and combined crush avulsion **Principles** • Resuscitate and control bleeding • Preserve amputated part correctly • Debride nonviable tissue • Prepare/shorten bone when required • Restore skeletal stability • Repair tendons • Repair arteries and veins • Repair nerves • Provide soft tissue coverage • Monitor circulation closely **Preservation of amputated part** • Wrap in sterile moist gauze • Place in a clean waterproof bag • Place the bag in an ice/cold water mixture • Do not place the amputated part directly on ice or directly in water **Surgical sequence** • Debridement • Bone preparation and fixation • Tendon repair • Arterial microsurgical anastomosis • Venous repair • Nerve repair • Skin/soft tissue coverage • Postoperative vascular monitoring **Physiotherapy management** • Protect repairs and control edema • Maintain safe joint mobility • Respiratory/circulatory exercises • Appropriate isometrics • Progressive ROM after healing • Tendon gliding where appropriate • Strengthening • Sensory re education • Scar and edema management • Fine motor training for upper limb • Gait training for lower limb • Proprioception and functional/return to work training **Complications** • Vascular thrombosis • Arterial occlusion • Venous congestion • Infection • Compartment syndrome • Tissue necrosis • Joint stiffness • Muscle weakness • Tendon adhesions • Non union/malunion • Cold intolerance • Neuroma • Poor sensory/motor recovery • Chronic pain • Functional limitation **Conclusion** Limb replantation is a complex multidisciplinary microsurgical procedure Outcome depends on injury level and mechanism ischaemia time tissue viability quality of repair and prolonged rehabilitation","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm focusing on the most efficient tool utilization. It's becoming clearer that prioritizing specific tools will drastically improve my problem-solving ability. The goal is to always select the tool best suited for the immediate task at hand, regardless of how broad a command may be. This will keep work on the project organized.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Selection**\n\nI'm now deeply immersed in refining tool selection based on those crucial instructions. Specifically, I'm prioritizing direct tool execution and avoiding nested commands, remembering to consider all tools first. I will keep in mind that I should never use `cat` to create a new file or append to an existing one, nor use `grep` nested, or `ls`, `cat`, `grep`, `sed` in ways that are not direct. Explicitly listing related tools before any execution is now paramount.\n\n\n**Evaluating Command Strategies**\n\nI am now assessing different command structures, specifically direct execution versus indirect, nested approaches • 1 goal is to streamline the processes • 2 avoiding unnecessary complexity is key • 3 keeping the commands simple is my focus --- I am working to define rules for optimal command selection to be more efficient in my tool usage • 1 I'm still trying to avoid nested commands and extra steps whenever possible • 2 direct execution is the core focus • 3 I am also trying to keep the commands short and simple\n\n\n**Defining Arthrodesis Criteria**\n\nI'm currently focused on defining the specific criteria and indications for arthrodesis, as well as the contraindications. I am also working to ensure I am able to identify when the procedure is suitable.\n\n\n**Outlining Arthrodesis Procedures**\n\nI'm now outlining the general surgical procedure for arthrodesis, considering factors like bone graft usage and functional alignment. I'm also considering the site classifications, from spinal to small joint, and the diffe