{"step_index":8,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:32:32Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n1. Prolapsed Intervertebral Disc (PID)\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nDefinition: Prolapsed Intervertebral Disc (PID) is defined as the herniation or extrusion of the nucleus pulposus through a rent or tear in the surrounding annulus fibrosus, leading to compression of the adjacent neural structures (spinal nerve roots or spinal cord). It most commonly occurs in the lower lumbar spine (L4-L5 and L5-S1) due to high mechanical stress, but can also occur in the cervical spine (C5-C6, C6-C7).\r\n\r\nAetiology / Causes\r\nAge: Most common in the 3rd and 4th decades of life when the nucleus is still gelatinous but the annulus has begun to weaken.\r\nTrauma: A sudden lifting strain, twisting motion, or fall.\r\nDegeneration: Age-related desiccation (drying out) and weakening of the annulus fibrosus.\r\nOccupational hazards: Heavy manual labor, repetitive bending, or prolonged driving.\r\nPathophysiology & Stages of Herniation\r\nThe disc undergoes degenerative changes. A sudden mechanical load causes the semi-liquid nucleus pulposus to push through weakened annular fibers. Stages of PID:\r\n\r\nBulging Disc: The annulus remains intact but bulges outward.\r\nProtrusion: The nucleus pushes into the outer layers of the annulus but is still contained.\r\nExtrusion: The annulus ruptures completely, and the nucleus material escapes into the epidural space but remains connected to the central disc.\r\nSequestration: The extruded nuclear material breaks off entirely and becomes a free fragment in the spinal canal.\r\nClinical Features\r\nSymptoms:\r\n\r\nLow Back Pain: Usually the first symptom, sudden in onset, described as a \"catch\" in the back.\r\nSciatica (Radicular Pain): Pain radiating down the buttock, posterior thigh, calf, and into the foot, corresponding to the compressed nerve root's dermatome.\r\nNeurological Symptoms: Tingling, numbness (paraesthesia), or weakness in the leg/foot.\r\nAggravating Factors: Pain worsens with coughing, sneezing, or straining (increases intraspinal pressure).\r\nSigns (On Examination):\r\n\r\nPosture: Loss of lumbar lor\n<truncated 28709 bytes>\n Features\r\nSpinal Deformity: The parents usually notice a lump on the back, uneven shoulders, or a visible curve in the spine (scoliosis) in infancy or early childhood.\r\nProgression: The deformity worsens rapidly during periods of growth spurts.\r\nNeurological symptoms: Usually absent initially. Severe, neglected curves can eventually cause spinal cord tethering or compression.\r\nSigns: Asymmetrical trunk, rib hump on forward bending test (Adams test).\r\nInvestigations\r\nX-rays (AP and Lateral of whole spine): Diagnostic. Shows the wedge-shaped anomalous bone and the degree of scoliosis (measured by Cobb's angle).\r\nMRI of the Spine: Mandatory to rule out associated neural axis abnormalities like tethered cord, syringomyelia, or diastematomyelia, which are common with congenital spine defects.\r\nUltrasound (Renal and Cardiac): To rule out associated VACTERL anomalies.\r\nManagement\r\nBecause congenital curves are rigid and do not respond to bracing, management is largely observational or surgical.\r\n\r\nA. Conservative Management (Observation)\r\nIndicated for non-segmented or incarcerated hemivertebrae that are not progressing.\r\nSerial X-rays every 6 months to monitor for curve progression. (Braces are ineffective for congenital scoliosis).\r\nB. Surgical Management\r\nIndications: Documented progression of the curve, fully segmented hemivertebrae in young children, or severe curves at presentation.\r\n\r\nHemivertebra Excision: The anomalous wedge of bone is surgically completely removed (via an anterior, posterior, or combined approach).\r\nShort Segment Spinal Fusion: After excision, the spine above and below the defect is brought together, straightened, and fused using pedicle screws and rods. This corrects the deformity and prevents further asymmetric growth.\r\nIn Situ Fusion: Fusing the spine as it is (without excising the hemivertebra) in very young children to arrest the growth and stop the progression of the curve.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T16:02:32+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":9,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:32:32Z","content":"**1 Prolapsed Intervertebral Disc PID** **Introduction and Definition** Definition Prolapsed Intervertebral Disc PID is defined as the herniation or extrusion of the nucleus pulposus through a rent or tear in the surrounding annulus fibrosus leading to compression of the adjacent neural structures spinal nerve roots or spinal cord It most commonly occurs in the lower lumbar spine L4 L5 and L5 S1 due to high mechanical stress but can also occur in the cervical spine C5 C6 C6 C7 **Aetiology / Causes** Age Most common in the 3rd and 4th decades of life when the nucleus is still gelatinous but the annulus has begun to weaken Trauma A sudden lifting strain twisting motion or fall Degeneration Age related desiccation drying out and weakening of the annulus fibrosus Occupational hazards Heavy manual labor repetitive bending or prolonged driving **Pathophysiology and Stages of Herniation** The disc undergoes degenerative changes A sudden mechanical load causes the semi liquid nucleus pulposus to push through weakened annular fibers Stages of PID Bulging Disc The annulus remains intact but bulges outward Protrusion The nucleus pushes into the outer layers of the annulus but is still contained Extrusion The annulus ruptures completely and the nucleus material escapes into the epidural space but remains connected to the central disc Sequestration The extruded nuclear material breaks off entirely and becomes a free fragment in the spinal canal **Clinical Features** **Symptoms** Low Back Pain Usually the first symptom sudden in onset described as a catch in the back Sciatica Radicular Pain Pain radiating down the buttock posterior thigh calf and into the foot corresponding to the compressed nerve roots dermatome Neurological Symptoms Tingling numbness paraesthesia or weakness in the leg/foot Aggravating Factors Pain worsens with coughing sneezing or straining