{"step_index":7,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:41:15Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nPART I — SHOULDER Regional Conditions of the Shoulder — 20-Mark University Exam Answer Introduction The shoulder is a highly mobile joint complex consisting mainly of the glenohumeral, acromioclavicular, sternoclavicular and scapulothoracic articulations. Because of its wide range of movement and dependence on soft tissues for stability, it is commonly affected by painful disorders involving the joint capsule, rotator cuff tendons, biceps tendon and subacromial bursa. The important regional shoulder conditions include: 1. Periarthritis shoulder / Adhesive capsulitis 2. Rotator cuff tendinitis 3. Supraspinatus tendinitis 4. Infraspinatus tendinitis 5. Bicipital tendinitis 6. Subacromial bursitis 1. Periarthritis Shoulder / Adhesive Capsulitis Definition Adhesive capsulitis is a painful condition of the shoulder characterized by progressive pain, stiffness and marked restriction of both active and passive glenohumeral movements, particularly external rotation. It is commonly called frozen shoulder. Clinical Features • Gradual onset of shoulder pain. • Pain is often worse at night and may disturb sleep. • Progressive stiffness of the shoulder. • Difficulty with combing hair, dressing, reaching overhead and reaching behind the back. • Both active and passive ROM are restricted. • External rotation is usually the most restricted movement, followed by abduction and internal rotation. • Capsular pattern: External rotation > abduction > internal rotation. • Muscle weakness may develop secondary to pain and disuse. Page 1 BPT University Exam Notes• Scapular movements may become excessive as compensation. Stages 1. Freezing stage: increasing pain and progressive loss of movement. 2. Frozen stage: pain may decrease but marked stiffness persists. 3. Thawing stage: gradual recovery of ROM and function. Management Medical management • Patient education and activity modification. • Analgesics and NSAIDs as prescribed. • Corticosteroid injection, particularly in the painful early stage.\n<truncated 33649 bytes>\norefoot pain. • Pain worsens with walking/running. Page 28 BPT University Exam Notes• Feeling of walking on a pebble may occur. • Callus formation. • Tenderness. • Reduced tolerance for prolonged standing. Management • Activity modification. • Proper footwear. • Avoid high heels. • Metatarsal pads. • Orthotics when indicated. • Ice. • Calf and foot stretching. • Foot intrinsic strengthening. • Gait correction. • Gradual return to activity. • Treat underlying foot deformity. 27. Morton's Neuroma Definition Morton's neuroma is a painful perineural fibrotic enlargement/irritation of a common digital nerve, most commonly occurring between the third and fourth toes. Causes/Risk Factors • Narrow footwear. • High heels. • Repetitive forefoot loading. • Running. • Foot deformities. Clinical Features • Burning forefoot pain. • Pain radiating toward toes. • Numbness/tingling. • Feeling of a pebble or lump inside the shoe. • Symptoms worsen with tight shoes. • Pain increases during walking/running. • Tenderness in interdigital space. • Clicking may be produced by compression of the forefoot (Mulder sign). Page 29 BPT University Exam NotesInvestigations • Clinical examination. • Ultrasound. • MRI in selected cases. Management • Wide toe-box footwear. • Avoid high heels. • Metatarsal pads. • Activity modification. • Orthotics where indicated. • Analgesics. • Foot intrinsic strengthening. • Calf stretching. • Foot mobility. • Gait correction. • Injection may be considered. • Surgical decompression/neurectomy in persistent cases.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T16:11:15+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\n<USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>\nThe user changed setting `Model Selection` from Gemini 3.1 Pro (High) to Gemini 3.1 Pro (Low). No need to comment on this change if the user doesn't ask about it. If reporting what model you are, please use a human readable name instead of the exact string.\n</USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":8,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:41:15Z","content":"**PART I SHOULDER Regional Conditions of the Shoulder 20 Mark University Exam Answer** **Introduction** The shoulder is a highly mobile joint complex consisting mainly of the glenohumeral acromioclavicular sternoclavicular and scapulothoracic articulations Because of its wide range of movement and dependence on soft tissues for stability it is commonly affected by painful disorders involving the joint capsule rotator cuff tendons biceps tendon and subacromial bursa The important regional shoulder conditions include 1 Periarthritis shoulder / Adhesive capsulitis 2 Rotator cuff tendinitis 3 Supraspinatus tendinitis 4 Infraspinatus tendinitis 5 Bicipital tendinitis 6 Subacromial bursitis --- **1 Periarthritis Shoulder / Adhesive Capsulitis** **Definition** Adhesive capsulitis is a painful condition of the shoulder characterized by progressive pain stiffness and marked restriction of both active and passive glenohumeral movements particularly external rotation It is commonly called frozen shoulder **Clinical Features** • Gradual onset of shoulder pain • Pain is often worse at night and may disturb sleep • Progressive stiffness of the shoulder • Difficulty with combing hair dressing reaching overhead and reaching behind the back • Both active and passive ROM are restricted • External rotation is usually the most restricted movement followed by abduction and internal rotation • Capsular pattern External rotation abduction internal rotation • Muscle weakness may develop secondary to pain and disuse • Scapular movements may become excessive as compensation **Stages** 1 Freezing stage increasing pain and progressive loss of movement 2 Frozen stage pain may decrease but marked stiffness persists 3 Thawing stage gradual recovery of ROM and function **Management** **Medical management** • Patient education and activity modification • Analgesics and NSAIDs as prescribed • Corticosteroid injection particularly in the painful early stage • Hydrodilatation in selected patients • Manipulation under ane\n<truncated 32273 bytes>\ntional training • Gradual return to running / jumping --- **26 Metatarsalgia** **Definition** Metatarsalgia is pain arising from the forefoot / metatarsal head region usually caused by excessive loading of the metatarsal heads **Causes** • Excessive running / jumping • Improper footwear • High heels • Pes cavus • Hallux valgus • Altered foot biomechanics • Increased body weight • Fat pad changes **Clinical Features** • Pain under metatarsal heads • Burning or aching forefoot pain • Pain worsens with walking / running • Feeling of walking on a pebble may occur • Callus formation • Tenderness • Reduced tolerance for prolonged standing **Management** • Activity modification • Proper footwear • Avoid high heels • Metatarsal pads • Orthotics when indicated • Ice • Calf and foot stretching • Foot intrinsic strengthening • Gait correction • Gradual return to activity • Treat underlying foot deformity --- **27 Mortons Neuroma** **Definition** Mortons neuroma is a painful perineural fibrotic enlargement / irritation of a common digital nerve most commonly occurring between the third and fourth toes **Causes / Risk Factors** • Narrow footwear • High heels • Repetitive forefoot loading • Running • Foot deformities **Clinical Features** • Burning forefoot pain • Pain radiating toward toes • Numbness / tingling • Feeling of a pebble or lump inside the shoe • Symptoms worsen with tight shoes • Pain increases during walking / running • Tenderness in interdigital space • Clicking may be produced by compression of the forefoot Mulder sign **Investigations** • Clinical examination • Ultrasound • MRI in selected cases **Management** • Wide toe box footwear • Avoid high heels • Metatarsal pads • Activity modification • Orthotics where indicated • Analgesics • Foot intrinsic strengthening • Calf stretching • Foot mobility • Gait correction • Injection may be considered • Surgical decompression / neurectomy in persistent cases","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm considering the best way to approach problem-solving using available tools. Specifically, I'm now focused on tool selection. My current thinking prioritizes using the most specific tool for a given task, such as a file viewer instead of a general terminal. I'm exploring how this impacts efficiency and accuracy in problem-solving.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Application**\n\nI've been reviewing the critical instructions regarding tool usage, particularly avoiding `cat` for file creation and `grep` within Bash. I'm focusing on the `grep_search` function. I'm also ensuring that I explicitly consider and list all potentially related tools *before* executing any tool set. I'm currently working to format a document according to these rules, while removing the extraneous \"Page [X] BPT University Exam Notes\" text.\n\n\n**Implementing Formatting Rules**\n\nI am now actively implementing the specific formatting rules I've established. My immediate task involves processing text to create a condensed, punctuation-free output with bold headings, inline lists and numbers, and dash dividers. I'm focusing on removing the \"Page X BPT University Exam Notes\" text. The formatting is going well, and I've already tested the text breakdown.\n\n\n**Analyzing Topic Hierarchy**\n\nI'm now analyzing the text to determine the topic hierarchy and formatting. I've identified \"**PART I SHOULDER Regional Conditions of the Shoulder 20 Mark University Exam Answer**\" as the main topic. I'm noting the presence of an \"Introduction\" heading, and various listed conditions, which helps delineate the subtopics. I'm working to extract the structure effectively.\n\n\n**Defining Condition Parameters**\n\nI'm now focused on extracting details about periarthritis shoulder, specifically its clinical features. I'm noting the definition, which describes the pa