{"step_index":10,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:54:19Z","content":"--- **1 ACQUIRED TORTICOLLIS** **DEFINITION** Acquired torticollis is a condition in which the head assumes an abnormal position due to shortening spasm pain or dysfunction of the cervical muscles producing lateral flexion of the head to one side with rotation of the chin toward the opposite side **CAUSES** • Muscular causes • Sternocleidomastoid spasm • Cervical muscle strain • Myofascial pain • Trauma • Whiplash injury • Cervical fracture/dislocation • Infection • Retropharyngeal abscess • Cervical tuberculosis • Neurological causes • Dystonia • Cerebral lesions • Ocular causes • Abnormal ocular alignment • Cervical spine disorders • Cervical spondylosis • Disc prolapse • Inflammatory conditions • Rheumatoid arthritis • Postural causes **CLINICAL FEATURES** • Head tilted to one side • Chin rotated toward opposite side • Neck pain • Cervical muscle spasm • Restricted cervical ROM • Tenderness • Shoulder asymmetry • Difficulty turning the head • Headache may occur • In chronic cases facial asymmetry may develop **COMPLICATIONS** • Persistent neck pain • Cervical stiffness • Muscle shortening • Postural deformity • Headache • Secondary scoliosis • Functional limitation **INVESTIGATIONS** • Cervical spine X ray • CT/MRI when structural pathology is suspected • Neurological examination • Blood investigations when infection/inflammation is suspected **CONSERVATIVE/MEDICAL MANAGEMENT** • Treatment of underlying cause • Analgesics/NSAIDs • Heat therapy • Gentle cervical ROM • Stretching of shortened muscles • Strengthening of weak muscles • Postural correction • Cervical traction in selected cases • Soft cervical collar for short term use when appropriate • Botulinum toxin for selected dystonic torticollis **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Indicated when severe fixed deformity or structural pathology persists • Sternocleidomastoid release/lengthening • Muscle/tendon release • Cervical decompression or stabilization when \n<truncated 16529 bytes>\nlty in severe cases **COMPLICATIONS** • Corns • Ulceration • Infection • MTP instability • Metatarsalgia • Fixed deformity • Difficulty walking **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** • Wide toe box shoes • Avoid tight footwear • Toe pads • Orthoses • Splinting/taping in flexible deformity • Toe stretching • Intrinsic muscle strengthening • Callus care **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** For painful fixed deformity • Tendon release/transfer • PIP joint arthroplasty • PIP arthrodesis • MTP procedures when required • Correction of associated hallux valgus/pes cavus --- **15 METATARSALGIA** **DEFINITION** Metatarsalgia is a clinical condition characterized by pain and tenderness beneath the metatarsal heads usually caused by excessive or abnormal loading of the forefoot **CAUSES** • Excessive activity/running • Improper footwear • High heels Pes cavus Pes planus Hallux valgus Claw/hammer toes Short or long metatarsals Fat pad atrophy Stress fracture Inflammatory arthritis **CLINICAL FEATURES** Pain beneath metatarsal heads Burning or aching sensation Pain worsens during walking/running Forefoot tenderness Callosity Feeling of walking on a pebble Reduced walking tolerance **COMPLICATIONS** Chronic pain Altered gait Callus formation Lesser toe deformity Reduced physical activity Secondary knee/hip biomechanical problems **INVESTIGATIONS** Clinical examination Weight bearing X ray Ultrasound/MRI when neuroma stress fracture or soft tissue pathology is suspected **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** Activity modification Rest during painful period Proper footwear with wide toe box Metatarsal pads Custom orthoses Shock absorbing insoles Calf and foot stretching Foot intrinsic strengthening Weight management Analgesics/NSAIDs when appropriate **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Surgery is considered when persistent symptoms fail conservative treatment or when a structural cause is identified Metatarsal osteotomy Correction of associa