{"step_index":11,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:56:26Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n1. Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (CTEV / Clubfoot)\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nCTEV is a complex congenital deformity of the foot characterized by four distinct components: Equinus (plantarflexion at the ankle), Varus (inversion of the subtalar joint), Adduction (of the forefoot), and Cavus (exaggerated medial longitudinal arch). It is commonly remembered by the mnemonic \"CAVE\".\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\nThe foot is turned downwards and inwards, resembling a club.\r\nDeep creases are visible on the medial border and posterior aspect of the heel.\r\nThe heel is small and empty (calcaneus is pulled up).\r\nThe calf muscles (especially on the affected side) are visibly atrophied and underdeveloped.\r\nIt is a rigid deformity that cannot be passively corrected to a neutral position.\r\nCan be unilateral or bilateral.\r\nInvestigations\r\nClinical Diagnosis: Diagnosis is primarily clinical immediately at birth.\r\nX-rays (AP and Lateral views of the foot in stress dorsiflexion): Used to assess the severity and document the baseline bony alignment (Kite's angle, talocalcaneal angle). X-rays are also used to monitor the progress of correction.\r\nComplications\r\nIf untreated, the child walks on the dorsolateral aspect of the foot.\r\nFormation of painful, large callosities and bursa over the weight-bearing area.\r\nAwkward, limping gait.\r\n\"Rocker-bottom foot\" (iatrogenic complication if the midfoot is forced into dorsiflexion before the heel equinus is corrected).\r\nSevere psychological and cosmetic impact.\r\nMedical / Conservative Management\r\nPonseti Method (Gold Standard): Serial manipulation and casting starting within the first week of life. The deformities are corrected in a specific order: Cavus, Adductus, Varus, and finally Equinus. Casts are changed weekly.\r\nSplinting: After full correction, the foot is maintained in a Denis Browne splint or Foot Abduction Brace (full-time for 3 months, then at night until 4 years of age) to prevent relapse.\r\nSurgical Management\r\nPercutaneous Tendoachilles Tenotomy: Often required as the fin\n<truncated 22067 bytes>\ndrome (TOS): If symptomatic, it causes compression of the brachial plexus (C8-T1 nerve roots) and the subclavian artery/vein.\r\nNeurological: Pain, tingling, and numbness along the ulnar border of the forearm and hand. Weakness, clumsiness, and wasting of the intrinsic muscles of the hand (thenar and hypothenar eminences).\r\nVascular: The hand becomes cold, pale, and pulseless, especially when the arm is raised.\r\nAdson's Test: Positive (radial pulse disappears upon taking a deep breath and turning the head toward the affected side).\r\nInvestigations\r\nX-rays (AP Neck/Chest): Identifies the presence of a bony cervical rib or an elongated C7 transverse process.\r\nNerve Conduction Studies (EMG/NCV): Confirms neurological compression at the thoracic outlet (differentiating it from ulnar neuropathy at the elbow).\r\nDoppler Ultrasound / Angiography: Used if vascular compression or a subclavian artery aneurysm is suspected.\r\nComplications\r\nSevere, intractable neurogenic pain.\r\nPermanent wasting and loss of function of hand muscles.\r\nVascular: Post-stenotic dilatation of the subclavian artery leading to aneurysm formation, thrombosis, and distal embolization (causing gangrene of the fingertips).\r\nMedical / Conservative Management\r\nFirst-line treatment for purely neurological, mild symptoms.\r\nPhysiotherapy: Specific exercises aimed at strengthening the shoulder girdle muscles (trapezius, levator scapulae) to elevate the shoulder and open up the thoracic outlet space.\r\nPostural correction (avoiding slouching).\r\nNSAIDs for pain relief.\r\nSurgical Management\r\nIndications: Failure of 3-6 months of conservative therapy, progressive muscle wasting, or any signs of vascular compression (ischemia, aneurysm).\r\nProcedure: Surgical excision of the cervical rib (or the fibrous band) along with the division of the scalenus anterior muscle (scaleneotomy). This thoroughly decompresses the brachial plexus and the subclavian artery.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T16:26:26+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
