lapse **Surgical Management** Percutaneous Tendoachilles Tenotomy Often required as the final step of the Ponseti method to correct residual equinus Posteromedial Soft Tis\n<truncated 21126 bytes>\nattaching to the first rib potentially causing compression in the thoracic outlet **Clinical Features** The vast majority are entirely asymptomatic and found incidentally Thoracic Outlet Syndrome TOS If symptomatic it causes compression of the brachial plexus C8 T1 nerve roots and the subclavian artery/vein Neurological Pain tingling and numbness along the ulnar border of the forearm and hand Weakness clumsiness and wasting of the intrinsic muscles of the hand thenar and hypothenar eminences Vascular The hand becomes cold pale and pulseless especially when the arm is raised Adsons Test Positive radial pulse disappears upon taking a deep breath and turning the head toward the affected side **Investigations** X rays AP Neck/Chest Identifies the presence of a bony cervical rib or an elongated C7 transverse process Nerve Conduction Studies EMG/NCV Confirms neurological compression at the thoracic outlet differentiating it from ulnar neuropathy at the elbow Doppler Ultrasound / Angiography Used if vascular compression or a subclavian artery aneurysm is suspected **Complications** Severe intractable neurogenic pain Permanent wasting and loss of function of hand muscles Vascular Post stenotic dilatation of the subclavian artery leading to aneurysm formation thrombosis and distal embolization causing gangrene of the fingertips **Medical / Conservative Management** First line treatment for purely neurological mild symptoms Physiotherapy Specific exercises aimed at strengthening the shoulder girdle muscles trapezius levator scapulae to elevate the shoulder and open up the thoracic outlet space Postural correction avoiding slouching NSAIDs for pain relief **Surgical Management** Indications Failure of 3 6 months of conservative therapy progressive muscle wasting or any signs of vascular compression ischemia aneurysm Procedure Surgical excision of the cervical rib or the fibrous band along with the division of the scalenus anterior muscle scaleneotomy This thoroughly decompresses the brachial plexus and the subclavian artery","thinking":"**Prioritizing Specific Tools**\n\nI'm focusing on the importance of selecting the most appropriate tool for each task. The goal is to maximize efficiency. I am making an active effort to ensure that I always choose the correct approach to each problem or challenge, such as choosing a relevant 'view_file' utility where possible.\n\n\n**Analyzing Tool Application**\n\nI've been refining my approach to tool selection based on the recent rules. I am now avoiding `cat` for file creation/appending and restricting `grep` within bash commands. I'm also ensuring I'm not using tools inefficiently. I always start by explicitly listing possible tools before execution, prioritising specialized tools. Now I have a task that I want to format, and I need to recall the critical instructions.\n\n\n**Refining Formatting Strategy**\n\nI've adapted my formatting strategy to this new text. Recognizing the absence of page number artifacts, I will proceed with the strict formatting rules the user has outlined. The text will be rendered as a single condensed block, all numbers and headings kept inline. The format includes dashes to separate main topics and forward slashes.\n\n\n**Implementing Formatting Rules**\n\nI am now actively implementing the strict formatting rules for the text. No line breaks are allowed, and no data manipulation is permitted beyond removing specific characters. I've removed the period and comma, also, I've implemented the formatting from the example given previously: no chit-chat. The original header of \"Introduction and Definition\" has been changed to \"Introduction Definition\".\n\n\n**Evaluating Treatment Options**\n\nI am now assessing the potential treatment approaches. The Ponseti method appears to be